  "body": "I noticed Edge, Chromium, and Firefox have conflicting behavior with regards to downloading scripts in a sandbox-ed `<iframe>`.  If a page has `<script src=\"foo.js\">` and is loaded inside a sandboxed frame where the script flag is set (i.e., script won't execute), FireFox (I presume correctly) avoids sending a network request to \"foo.js\" (this is based on the developer tools tab on networking which I trusted shows all the requests from the client). Edge and Chromium however do send requests.\r\n\r\nIIUC, [the spec](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/origin.html#sandboxed-scripts-browsing-context-flag) only mentions blocking execution in this case.\r\n\r\nOn a related note I see an open issue #3230 which suggests `script-src 'none'` should disable scripting. I'd argue that perhaps when scripting is disabled in a frame, it should behave like what issue #3230 claims `script-src 'none'` should?\r\n\r\nPS: If there is explicit spec statements on loading scripts in sandbox frames that I have missed, then one of the browsers above has a bug, but if not I think this might be something worth fixing in the spec.",
